---
/**
 * Methods: the two verification profiles, the measured counts, what is NOT RUN and why,
 * the unit conventions, and the data and provenance policy. F09 is the exhibit.
 *
 * The status table below carries five states and uses all of them. NOT RUN, BLOCKED and
 * NOT APPLICABLE are not degraded forms of PASS; they are the honest outcome for a check
 * whose preconditions are absent, and this page depends on being able to say so.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import DataTable from "../../components/DataTable.astro";
import StatusTable from "../../components/StatusTable.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import { checkedExhibit, provenance, unitsLine } from "../../scripts/exhibits";
import { download, dataIndex } from "../../scripts/public-data";
import { NAV, VERIFICATION, NOT_RUN, GATE, FOUR_STATEMENTS } from "../../scripts/site";
import { loadContract } from "../../lib/figures";
import { href } from "../../lib/urls";

const f09 = await checkedExhibit("F09");
const contract = loadContract();
const prov = provenance();
const idx = dataIndex();
const contractDownload = await download("contract.json");

const digestRows = prov.dataFileDigests.map((d) => ({
  file: d.file,
  declared: d.declared,
  match: d.match ? "yes" : "no",
}));

const unitRows = Object.entries(contract.units).map(([k, v]) => ({
  q: k.replace(/_/g, " "),
  v,
}));

const contents = [
  { id: "profiles", label: "Two verification profiles" },
  { id: "measured", label: "What was measured" },
  { id: "notrun", label: "What is NOT RUN, and why" },
  { id: "units", label: "Unit conventions" },
  { id: "provenance", label: "Data and provenance policy" },
  { id: "checking", label: "Checking it yourself" },
];

const description =
  "The two verification profiles, the measured test and check counts, what is deliberately not run and why, the unit conventions, and the data and provenance policy.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methods"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/methods/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <h1>Methods</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    This page is the record of what has actually been checked, what has not, and how to
    reproduce either. It is written to be falsifiable: every number on it came from running a
    command on a named date on a named interpreter, and the commands are here so that a
    reader can disagree by running them.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Two claims this page deliberately does not make:</strong> that the library has
      been validated against field data, and that a hosted continuous-integration service has
      run anything. Neither has happened.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section id="profiles" aria-labelledby="profiles-h">
    <h2 id="profiles-h">Two verification profiles</h2>

    <p>
      Verification runs at one of two depths, and the difference is worth a name because it
      is the difference between "this code agrees with itself" and "this code agrees with
      something it did not produce".
    </p>

    <h3>public-core</h3>
    <p>
      Everything a clone of the public tree can run, with no restricted files and no network.
      It covers the numerical core, the analytic and closed-form identities, the dependency
      policy, and the synthetic studies, which it re-runs from scratch and compares against
      their committed snapshots byte for byte.
    </p>
    <pre><code>python3 scripts/verify.py --profile public-core</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>This is a self-consistency and reproducibility result.</strong> The code agrees
      with its own committed snapshots, its own analytic limits and its own closed forms, and
      it has not drifted. {NOT_RUN.externalValidation}
    </p>

    <h3>external-reference</h3>
    <p>
      An explicit opt-in for a reader who has obtained the reference extract themselves. It
      is the only profile that compares the library against values it did not produce, and it
      requires both the path to the extract and a stated access basis, failing clearly without
      either. The access basis is recorded in the report on purpose: access rights and
      redistribution rights are different rights.
    </p>
    <pre><code>python3 scripts/verify.py --profile external-reference \
    --reference-dir /path/to/your/extract \
    --access-basis "how you came to hold this data"</code></pre>
    <p>
      It refuses to move data for you. If your copy lives elsewhere, the profile verifies its
      digests against the committed manifest, reports the reference-dependent tests as not
      run, and tells you to place or symlink the files yourself. See the
      {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>A2 availability page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="measured" aria-labelledby="measured-h">
    <h2 id="measured-h">What was measured</h2>

    <p>
      Measured on {VERIFICATION.date} with {VERIFICATION.interpreter}, and re-measured in this
      candidate tree before publication. The two test runners disagree by exactly the skip
      count, because one counts a skipped test in its total and the other does not:
      {" "}{VERIFICATION.testsCollected} minus {VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} is
      {" "}{VERIFICATION.testsPassed}.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      caption={`Public-core profile, measured on ${VERIFICATION.date}. These are counts from a run, not targets.`}
      captionTitle="Measured"
      columns={[
        { key: "q", label: "Quantity", rowHeader: true },
        { key: "v", label: "Measured", numeric: true },
      ]}
      rows={[
        { q: "tests collected", v: VERIFICATION.testsCollected },
        { q: "tests passed", v: VERIFICATION.testsPassed },
        { q: "tests failed or errored", v: VERIFICATION.testsFailedOrErrored },
        { q: "tests skipped", v: VERIFICATION.testsSkipped },
        { q: "reference-dependent tests, not run", v: VERIFICATION.testsSkipped },
        { q: "checks collected", v: VERIFICATION.checksCollected },
        { q: "checks passed", v: VERIFICATION.checksPassed },
        { q: "mandatory checks failed", v: VERIFICATION.mandatoryChecksFailed },
        { q: "synthetic studies reproduced", v: VERIFICATION.casesReproduced },
        { q: "figure data files re-exported and diffed", v: VERIFICATION.figureDataFiles },
        { q: "exporter reconciliation checks recorded", v: VERIFICATION.reconciliationChecks },
        { q: "exporter reconciliation tolerance", v: VERIFICATION.reconcileTolerance },
      ]}
      id="t-measured"
    />

    <p>
      There is no composite score anywhere on this site and no "N of M passing" badge. A
      single number over a set of checks invites exactly the substitution the flagship study
      is about: reading one aggregate statistic as if it answered a question about a quantity
      it does not measure.
    </p>

    <StatusTable
      caption="Every check, what it covers, where it ran, and what it actually did."
      captionTitle="Verification"
      rows={[
        {
          check: "Unit and property tests",
          scope: `${VERIFICATION.testsCollected} collected across the numerical core, the analytic identities and the dependency policy`,
          environment: VERIFICATION.interpreter,
          state: "PASS",
          note: `${VERIFICATION.testsPassed} passed, ${VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} skipped, 0 failed or errored`,
        },
        {
          check: "Study reproduction",
          scope: "A1, A3 and A4 re-run into fresh directories and diffed against their committed snapshots",
          environment: VERIFICATION.interpreter,
          state: "PASS",
          note: "byte-identical in all three",
          evidence: { label: "Studies", path: "/studies/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Figure-data re-export",
          scope: `${VERIFICATION.figureDataFiles} figure files plus the export contract, re-exported to a scratch directory and diffed`,
          environment: VERIFICATION.interpreter,
          state: "PASS",
          note: `byte-identical, with ${VERIFICATION.reconciliationChecks} reconciliation checks recorded by the exporter at ${VERIFICATION.reconcileTolerance}`,
          evidence: { label: "contract.json", path: "/data/contract.json" },
        },
        {
          check: "Export-contract digests at site build",
          scope: "Every figure data file re-hashed against contract.json while this page was built",
          environment: "this site build",
          state: "PASS",
          note: "a mismatch stops the build; no page can render a number from a file that does not match its declared digest",
        },
        {
          check: "Download integrity at site build",
          scope: `${idx.count} published files re-hashed against their recorded SHA-256`,
          environment: "this site build",
          state: "PASS",
          note: "a stale or hand-edited download cannot ship under a name that implies it came from the committed export",
        },
        {
          check: "Reference-dependent tests",
          scope: `${VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} tests in the gas-properties suite`,
          environment: "not run in the public profile",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "the reference extract is not redistributed; the skips are expected and their count is measured, not asserted",
          evidence: { label: "A2 availability", path: "/studies/a2/" },
        },
        {
          check: "A2 study",
          scope: "The one study that reads the reference tables directly",
          environment: "not run in the public profile",
          state: "BLOCKED",
          note: "exits 1 when the tables are absent, which is correct behaviour and not a defect",
          evidence: { label: "A2 availability", path: "/studies/a2/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Digest verification of the reference tables",
          scope: "SHA-256 of three extracts against the committed manifest",
          environment: "not applicable in the public profile",
          state: "NOT APPLICABLE",
          note: "hashing a file the tree is supposed not to have would fail by design; the manifest is validated as a provenance record instead",
        },
        {
          check: "Hosted continuous integration",
          scope: "Any check running anywhere other than the author's machine",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "no hosted run is reported here; every check above ran on the author's own machine",
        },
        {
          check: "Deployment",
          scope: "Publication of this site to any origin",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "the deployment target is configuration; with no origin supplied the build omits the canonical link and the social block rather than inventing a domain",
        },
        {
          check: "External validation against field data",
          scope: "Comparison of any result here against a measured reservoir",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT APPLICABLE",
          note: "there is no field data in this project and none is implied; every history is synthetic",
        },
        {
          check: "Human peer review",
          scope: "Review by a person other than the author",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "every review pass was conducted under a different role by the same author",
          evidence: { label: "About", path: "/about/" },
        },
      ]}
      id="verification-status"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="notrun" aria-labelledby="notrun-h">
    <h2 id="notrun-h">What is NOT RUN, and why</h2>

    <p>
      The profile reports these rather than skipping them quietly. A count of zero tests run
      and a count of twenty tests deliberately not run are different situations, and a report
      that shows only green cannot tell them apart.
    </p>

    <dl class="reasons">
      <dt>{VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} tests in the gas-properties suite</dt>
      <dd>
        They compare computed deviation factors, densities and viscosities against reference
        tables that are absent from the public tree on purpose: they are someone else's data,
        and access to them is not the same as the right to redistribute them. The test oracle
        skips them with a reason that names the missing directory. The count is measured — the
        verification runner counts skips carrying a reference-absent sentinel and fails on any
        skip the reference policy does not explain — and the expected value passed on the
        command line is a drift alarm on top of that measurement, not the source of it.
      </dd>

      <dt>The A2 study</dt>
      <dd>
        It declares the reference tables as run inputs and exits non-zero when they are
        absent. It ships no committed summary, which is how the verification runner tells it
        apart from the reproducible studies without carrying a hard-coded list.
      </dd>

      <dt>Digest verification of those tables</dt>
      <dd>
        Hashing a file the tree is supposed not to have would fail by design and would tell a
        reader nothing, so the public check validates the manifest as a provenance record
        instead: required fields present, digests well formed, the decision recorded.
      </dd>

      <dt>Hosted continuous integration</dt>
      <dd>{NOT_RUN.hostedCi}</dd>

      <dt>Deployment</dt>
      <dd>{NOT_RUN.deployment}</dd>

      <dt>Human peer review</dt>
      <dd>{NOT_RUN.peerReview}</dd>
    </dl>

    <Callout kind="note" title="The four statements, and what clearing a gate means">
      <ol>
        {FOUR_STATEMENTS.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{s.name}</strong> — {s.text}.
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p>
        The 1 percent figure that appears in the flagship study is a borrowed project
        demonstration criterion. {GATE.scope} {GATE.meaning}
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="units" aria-labelledby="units-h">
    <h2 id="units-h">Unit conventions</h2>

    <p>
      Declared once in the export contract and printed on every figure, because a reader who
      misreads Bscf as scf misreads a finding by nine orders of magnitude.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      caption="Units and standard conditions, as contract.json declares them."
      captionTitle="Units"
      columns={[
        { key: "q", label: "Quantity", rowHeader: true },
        { key: "v", label: "Convention" },
      ]}
      rows={unitRows}
      id="t-units"
    />

    <p class="units-line">{unitsLine()}</p>

    <p>
      Quantities are kept apart as carefully as units are. Inventory in place, recoverable
      gas and reserves are three different things: recoverable gas is smaller than inventory
      by whatever the drive mechanism and the abandonment condition leave behind, and reserves
      are a further commercial and regulatory classification of a recoverable volume. Nothing
      on this site is presented as any of the other two. Cumulative produced water, its
      reservoir-volume equivalent and total influx are likewise three quantities with two
      conversions between them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="provenance" aria-labelledby="provenance-h">
    <h2 id="provenance-h">Data and provenance policy</h2>

    <Figure
      id={f09.id}
      number={f09.number}
      title={f09.title}
      svg={f09.svg}
      units={f09.units}
      interpretation="Every number on this site traces to a committed file through a declared digest, and the chain from the study's run summary to the exported figure data to this page is drawn here in full."
      caveat={f09.caveat}
      longDescription={f09.longDescription}
      evidenceClass={f09.evidenceClass}
      tableLabel="Data table — the declared digests"
      tableOpen={true}
      svgDownload={f09.svgDownload}
      dataDownload={contractDownload.path}
      dataDownloadLabel={contractDownload.name}
    >
      <DataTable
        caption="Figure data files and the SHA-256 each one is declared at in contract.json. The site build re-hashes every file it reads and stops on a mismatch."
        captionTitle="Table F09"
        columns={[
          { key: "file", label: "File", rowHeader: true },
          { key: "declared", label: "Declared SHA-256" },
          { key: "match", label: "Matched when the figures were rendered" },
        ]}
        rows={digestRows}
        id="t-f09"
      />
      <Fragment slot="footnote">
        <p>
          The numerical source is the study's audited run summary at
          {" "}<code>{prov.numericalSource.baseline_summary_path}</code>, declared at
          {" "}<code>{prov.numericalSource.baseline_summary_sha256}</code>. The exporter is
          {" "}<code>{prov.exportBlock.exporter_path}</code>, declared at
          {" "}<code>{prov.exportBlock.exporter_sha256}</code>. The reconciliation tolerance is a
          same-environment determinism target, not an independent reproduction.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </Figure>

    <h3>Published data</h3>
    <p>
      {idx.count} files, {(idx.bytes / 1000).toFixed(0)} kB in total, generated from the
      committed export by <code>{idx.generator}</code>. CSV copies are written at
      {" "}{idx.csv_precision}. Full-dataset and selected-case downloads are separate files with
      separate names, so a reader who takes a file from the scenario explorer can tell from
      the file name which of the two they hold.
    </p>
    <p><strong>{idx.excluded}</strong></p>

    <h3>Provenance, stated exactly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Studies A2 and A3</strong> declare their protocol as a run input and pin it by
        SHA-256 inside a record the run itself wrote. That is internal content linkage as
        recorded. It is <em>not</em> an independent trusted timestamp and <em>not</em> proof
        against retrospective construction.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Studies A1 and A4</strong> declare no inputs, so nothing machine-written pins
        their protocol to their runs, and their prospective ordering is unverified. The studies
        say so themselves.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every date in this repository is self-reported.</strong> No signed tag and no
        trusted timestamp attests to any of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shipped run records are not edited after the fact</strong>, including where a
        later audit found a stale sentence in one. A contemporaneous machine-written record is
        more useful wrong than corrected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Superseded claims stay visible and stay marked.</strong> They are not quietly
        rewritten; the correction and its direction are shown next to the claim it replaced.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="checking" aria-labelledby="checking-h">
    <h2 id="checking-h">Checking it yourself</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>Re-run a study into a fresh directory and diff its summary against the committed one.</li>
      <li>Re-export the figure data and diff the {VERIFICATION.figureDataFiles} files against what is committed under the site's data directory.</li>
      <li>Hash any published download and compare it with the SHA-256 recorded in the data index.</li>
      <li>Read the export contract, which carries the digests of the run summary, the study source and the exporter itself.</li>
      <li>Rebuild this site. A missing figure, a stale figure, a data file that disagrees with its digest, a missing download or a hand-written site-absolute URL each stop the build rather than degrading the page.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      The last one is not decoration. This site has no runtime charting, no client-side data
      fetch and no placeholder path: a page that could not render an exhibit does not get
      built, because a reader would otherwise have no way to tell an exhibit that was withdrawn
      from one that failed to build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .reasons dt {
      font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
      margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    }
    .reasons dd {
      margin-inline-start: 0;
      padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
      border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--c-rule);
    }
    .units-line {
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--c-ink-2);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
